<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wuyue</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 978 CE · capital Qiantang (Hangzhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A peaceful and prosperous coastal kingdom around Hangzhou, famous for its Buddhist pagodas and sea trade, that handed itself over to the Song without a fight.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qian-liu">Qian Liu</a>, a former salt smuggler who became a soldier, defeated the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-chang">Dong Chang</a> in 896 and gained control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> coast. In 907 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> named him Prince of Wuyue, and he governed from Qiantang (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Qian rulers generally accepted the suzerainty of the northern dynasties, avoiding major wars except with their neighbour <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wu-ten-kingdoms">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</a> and its successor <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a>. They built sea walls along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang river</a>, drained and irrigated land, and traded by sea with the Khitan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Wuyue was a great centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leifeng-pagoda">Leifeng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baochu-pagoda">Baochu</a> pagodas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> date from its rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qian-chu">Qian Chu</a>, the last king, helped the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> conquer <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a> and in 978 surrendered his whole kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> without bloodshed. The Qian family was honoured at the Song court, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-song">Southern Song</a> capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wuyue.jpg" alt="Wuyue" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 978 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qiantang (Hangzhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qian Liu, Qian Yuanguan, Qian Chu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Wu Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wu-Yue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=wuyue" aria-label="Wuyue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7798 1073 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wuyue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8118 1329L7855 1179M8118 1329L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8118 1329h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8118 1329h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=wuyue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
